25:  Background and Objectives: Milk is an important component of the human diet. Exposure to heavy metals through
26:  dairy consumption of contaminated foods endangers human health. The aim of this study was to assess concentrations of
27:  lead and cadmium in raw milk samples from east of Iran and their safety risks.
28:  Materials and Methods: In this study, 54 raw milk samples from five regions were selected by cluster sampling and 20
29:  samples of various popular brands of pasteurized milks were randomly purchased from supermarkets in east of Iran. Samples
30:  were digested in laboratory using acid digestion method. Concentrations of elements were calculated using graphite furnace
32:  noncarcinogenic effects of lead and cadmium.
33:  Results: The mean concentrations of lead and cadmium in raw milks included 38.15 and 4.67 ±0.001 μg/kg, respectively.
34:  The mean concentrations of lead and cadmium in pasteurized milk included 48.49 ±0.001 and 6.84 ±0.001 μg/kg,
36:  limits. There were hence no possible risks of exposure to lead and cadmium as results of raw and pasteurized milk
38:  Conclusions: Lead and cadmium levels did not exceed maximum levels (MLs) in any samples of raw and pasteurized
39:  milks. Persistent monitoring is a critical action to protect consumers from contaminated foods. The health risk assessment
42:  Keywords: Lead, Cadmium, Heavy metals, Raw milk, Pasteurized milk, East of Iran
