                  3 Results and discussion
                  The detected concentrations of heavy metals studied in carrots and cucumbers sampled
                  at the sampling points are shown in Table 1. The average concentrations of Pb, Cd, Cr,
                  and Ni in the studied carrot samples were 0.303, 0.004, 0.654, and 0.409 mg kg− 1, respec-
                  tively. While the concentrations of Pb, Cd, Cr, and Ni in the studied cucumber samples
                  were 0.075, 0.003, 0.175, and 0.136 mg kg− 1, respectively. So, the order of the studied
                  metals in carrots and cucumber was Cr > Ni > Pb > Cd. As shown in Fig. 2, the concentra-
                  tions of the studied metals were not the same in the different samples. The total concen-
                  tration of the studied metals in the carrot samples was 1.296–1.483 mg kg− 1, while the
                  total concentration of the studied metals in the cucumber samples was 0.343–0.474 mg
                  kg− 1. Also, the highest concentration of total studied heavy metals in carrot and cucum-
                  ber samples was observed in SP2 and SP1, respectively. The results showed that the aver-
                  age concentration of the total metals studied in carrots and cucumbers was 1.37 and
                  0.39 mg kg− 1, respectively, indicating that the concentration of heavy metals in the root
                  vegetables was 3.51 times higher than in the fruit vegetables.
                    The concentration of heavy metals in the studied carrot and cucumber samples was
                  higher than the standard in some cases. The standard of Pb concentration in cucum-
                  ber and carrot is 0.1 mg kg− 1, while the standard of Cd concentration in cucumber and

