                  carrot is 0.05 and 0.1 mg kg− 1, respectively [36]. Therefore, the detected concentration of
                  Pb in carrot samples was higher than the standard and in cucumber samples was lower
                  than the standard, while the concentration of Cd in carrot and cucumber samples was
                  lower than the standard. Also, the standard concentration of Ni and Cr in the studied
                  vegetables has been suggested as 1.5 and 0.5 mg kg− 1, respectively [36, 43]. Therefore,
                  the detected concentration of Ni in carrot and cucumber samples was lower than the
                  standard, while the concentration of Cr in carrot samples was higher than the standard
                  and in cucumber samples was lower than the standard. Contamination of agricultural
                  products, including vegetables, can be an important factor in food safety [44]. Although
                  food quantity and access to food are known as the main criteria for food safety, contami-
                  nation can cause food to be removed from the consumption cycle and affect food safety
                  [45]. Therefore, the detected concentration of lead and chromium in carrots can limit
                  their consumption. The removal of food items such as vegetables from the food chain
                  due to heavy metal contamination can lead to food loss, which has a significant impact
                  on the production of food waste as an environmental risk [46, 47]. Therefore, a food con-
                  tamination control program is needed to reduce food loss, which aims to reduce con-
                  tamination in food and its related diseases [48].
                    The concentrations of detected heavy metals in carrot and cucumber samples in this
                  study can be compared with the levels of contamination in agricultural products in other
                  regions of Iran. Zafarzadeh et al. 2018, reported the concentrations of Zn, Pb, Cd, and
Mohammadi et al. Discover Sustainability   (2025) 6:966                                                            Page 7 of 13




                  Cu in tomatoes and cucumbers in northern Iran, which showed that the concentrations
                  of Pb and Cd were 7 to 14 times higher than the standard, while the concentrations of
                  Zn and Cu were lower than the standard [38]. In another example, Selahvarzi et al. 2022,
                  reported the average concentrations of Cd, Cu, Pb, and Zn in potatoes analyzed in cen-
                  tral Iran (Lorestan province) as 0.154, 0.148, 0.250 and 0.143 mg kg− 1, respectively [49].
                  The higher concentration of Cd and Pb in potatoes (tuber vegetables) than in cucumbers
                  in our study, as well as the higher concentration of Cd in potatoes than in carrots in our
