                  organizations such as the World Health Organization, which has led to the recom-
                  mendation of consuming more vegetables in the diet [5]. Therefore, due to population
                  growth in recent decades and economic growth in many countries, the need for more
                  vegetables has led to the development of agricultural land.
                    The development of agricultural land in recent decades, along with industrialization
                  and urbanization, especially in developing countries, has increased the risk of soil and
                  agricultural products contamination due to environmental pollution [6, 7]. Sources of
                  pollutant emissions into the environment, such as industrial and municipal wastewa-
                  ter and solid waste, can contaminate soil and water and cause the accumulation of pol-
                  lutants in agricultural products, including vegetables [8–10]. In developing countries
                  such as Iran, the risk of contamination of agricultural products has increased in recent
                  decades due to the expansion of industries and cities in the nearby of agricultural lands
                  [7]. Industrial development and urbanization, especially in areas that have traditionally
                  had thriving agriculture, can increase pollution in agricultural products. For example,
                  Khuzestan Province in southern Iran, which has significant agricultural land, has faced
                  increasing environmental pollution in recent years due to industrial development, popu-
                  lation growth, and urbanization [11–13]. Therefore, the increase in sources of land and
                  water pollution in recent decades and the increase in per capita consumption of veg-
                  etables as one of the main agricultural products in developing countries can have health
                  consequences resulting from the consumption of contaminated vegetables.
                    Heavy metals are one of the environmental concerns arising from pollution emission
                  sources that have various health consequences for humans [14, 15]. Heavy metal con-
                  centrations in water sources are a serious concern that has been reported in many sur-
                  face waters [16]. Although exposure to heavy metals has been reported even through
                  inhalation of particulate matter in the air [17], concentrations of heavy metals in water
                  can cause serious exposure through the food chain. In this situation, the contamination
                  reported in soil in several studies [18, 19] could exacerbate the concentration of heavy
                  metals in the food chain. Some heavy metals such as iron, zinc, copper, and magnesium
                  are known as micronutrients in minor concentrations that must be supplied in the diet
                  [20]. However, increasing the concentration of this group of heavy metals that called
                  essential metals, in food and exposure to them above the standards recommended by
                  international organizations such as the World Health Organization can lead to related
                  diseases [21]. But some heavy metals, called non-essential metals, such as chromium,
                  cadmium, lead, and mercury, can also pose a health risk to humans even in low concen-
                  trations [22]. In addition to the toxicity of heavy metals and their health consequences,
                  the properties of these elements, including their persistence and bioaccumulation in
                  plants, seafood, animals, and the food chain, are also important [23, 24]. So, heavy met-
                  als are of major concern regarding nutrition-related health risks due to their proven
                  health consequences and accumulation in agricultural products, including vegetables
                  [25].
                    The concentration of heavy metals in agricultural products, including vegetables,
                  depends on the concentration of pollutants in soil and water, as well as the type of veg-
                  etables [26, 27]. In recent decades, industrial development and urbanization have been
                  factors affecting the increase in the emission of various pollutants, including heavy met-
                  als [28]. Especially in developing countries, several studies have reported the concen-
                  tration of heavy metals in soil and water [29, 30], which can be considered as a risk of
Mohammadi et al. Discover Sustainability        (2025) 6:966                                                      Page 3 of 13




                  increasing these pollutants in agricultural products. Therefore, consumption of foods
                  containing heavy metals can lead to health risks for consumers, evidence of which has
                  been reported in various studies [31]. Even the health risk associated with heavy met-
                  als in food can increase the probability of cancer in society [32]. Scientific evidence of
                  health risks from exposure to heavy metals through various routes, including food and
                  inhalation, has been reported in recent years, responding to concerns about increasing
                  concentrations of heavy metals in the environment [33, 34]. Increasing the concentra-

